    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WSU_Jobs/job/Spokane-WA/Vice-Chair--Emergency-Medicine---College-of-Medicine_R-15517": {
      "title": "Vice Chair, Emergency Medicine | College of Medicine",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: November 15, 2026 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 155-NN_FACULTY - Research Assistant Professor - Career, 156-NN_FACULTY - Research Associate Professor - Career, 157-NN_FACULTY - Research Professor - Career, 158-NN_FACULTY - Teaching Assistant Professor - Career, 159-NN_FACULTY - Teaching Associate Professor - Career, 160-NN_FACULTY - Teaching Professor - Career, 161-NN_FACULTY - Scholarly Assistant Professor - Career, 162-NN_FACULTY - Scholarly Associate Professor - Career, 163-NN_FACULTY - Scholarly Professor - Career, 164-NN_FACULTY - Clinical Assistant Professor - Career, 165-NN_FACULTY - Clinical Associate Professor - Career, 166-NN_FACULTY - Clinical Professor - Career Business Title: Vice Chair, Emergency Medicine | College of Medicine Employee Type: Faculty Position Term: 12 Month Position Details: The Elson S. Floyd College of Medicine (ESFCOM) at Washington State University is seeking a Vice Chair for Emergency Medicine . As the Vice Chair for Emergency Medicine, you will report to the Chair of the Department of Medical Education & Clinical Sciences (DMECS) and serve as the college’s thought leader for the development, design, coordination, and implementation of the Emergency Medicine curriculum.   You will foster a positive and inclusive academic environment for learners and faculty, assure comparability of educational experiences and learner assessment across campuses, and collaborate with college leadership and stakeholders to integrate Emergency Medicine across medical education, clinical practice, and scholarship and to create clinical education experiences that add significant value to learning and competency.   The position allows flexibility to accommodate the incumbent's clinical activities with a mutually agreed upon FTE with a negotiable range of .6 - 1.0 FTE. You will hold a 12-month, full-time, continuous career track (subtrack determined at time of hire) position in the Department of Medical Education and Clinical Sciences with an academic rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor commensurate with qualifications. This position may be based at any of the Elson S. Floyd College of Medicine Clinical Campuses in WA State such as Spokane, Tri-Cities, Vancouver or Everett.   Please see below links for rank guidelines: Faculty Manual Section 3.C.5 College of Medicine Tenure and Promotion Guidelines Essential Functions   Educator Role | 40% Integrate the curricular and assessment framework for Emergency Medicine vertically and horizontally across the four years of the MD Program , in partnership with the Associate Dean for Undergraduate Medical Education (ADUME) including: Developing learning objectives, activities, and assessment methodologies that meet MD Program outcomes, with appropriate spiraling of content and alignment with developmental milestones. Advising students who are having difficulty achieving expected learning outcomes and working with faculty on targeted skills development and remediation plans. Holding primary responsibility for the fourth-year core Emergency Medicine course, including didactic and clinical curriculum design, development, approval, assessment, standardization, and implementation. Designing and approving elective experiences, leading or co-leading courses as required, and teaching across all four years of the curriculum. Monitoring course and program evaluations and aggregate student performance with the Curriculum Committee and its subcommittees. Reviewing and finalizing outward-facing educational materials for learners and faculty in the discipline.   Assure quality and comparability at every campus, in partnership with the Regional Associate Deans (RAD) and ADUME, including: Monitoring and supervising curriculum delivery, educational quality, and learner outcomes across campuses, learning environments, and affiliate sites. Leading specialty-specific orientation and faculty development for teaching faculty and preceptors, including CME opportunities, in coordination with the ADFD and other Vice Chairs. Supporting teaching quality improvement and ensuring compliance with educational accreditation standards.   DMECS Leadership | 35% Support the development and direction of Emergency Medicine graduate medical education programs, in partnership with the Associate Dean for Graduate Medical Education (ADGME)   Support Emergency Medicine faculty and others in an assigned portfolio, including: Recruiting, interviewing, and onboarding faculty across all campuses and affiliate sites, and supporting faculty retention and succession planning. Recruiting, developing, and supporting Emergency Medicine clinical practice partner groups. Formulating, supporting, and moderating faculty enhancement and remediation when consulted. Conducting annual faculty performance evaluations and mid-year check-ins, including review of academic productivity and professional development goals. Mentoring and coaching faculty, establishing individualized academic development plans, and guiding career planning, navigation of departmental structure, promotion and tenure pathways, and dossier preparation. Addressing faculty concerns and facilitating problem-solving and conflict resolution. Serving as the primary communication link between departmental leadership and faculty, including meeting with faculty individually and in groups at least quarterly, communicating departmental goals and changes, facilitating feedback, and monitoring morale and engagement.   Support the research agenda of the college in Emergency Medicine and medical education, including: Using a scholarly, evidence-based approach to teaching and learning and staying abreast of medical education trends and best practices in the specialty. Developing and implementing research related to the discipline. Supporting and directing research, publications, presentations, and educational scholarship for faculty and Emergency Medicine–bound students, including student scholarly projects and promotion-related scholarly activities.   Provide departmental leadership and strategic direction in partnership with the DMECS Chair, including: Participating in departmental leadership meetings and assisting the Chair with strategic planning, policy development, and operational decision-making. Participating in leadership initiatives across affiliated health systems. Identifying opportunities for departmental growth, developing new programs and services, leading special projects, and developing business cases and resource requests. Supporting the faculty practice plan, including innovative integration with medical education. Supporting quality, patient safety, and performance improvement initiatives, including review of performance and operational metrics and collaboration with departmental and organizational quality leaders.   Demonstrate service and citizenship to the department, college, university, state, and professional organizations, including: Serving as the external face of and content expert for Emergency Medicine for the college and university. Representing the specialty, department, and college on departmental, medical staff, hospital, university, and cross-disciplinary committees, workgroups, and task forces, and leading projects as assigned. Modeling professionalism and leadership, fostering diversity, inclusion, and inter-professionalism, and promoting a positive work and learning environment in all academic and clinical settings. Building strong relationships with COM stakeholders and partnering with the development director to support fundraising initiatives. Traveling to the COM’s campuses and faculty practices throughout the state as needed to support comparability, relationships, faculty development, and recruitment.   Student Support | 20% Support student success through Emergency Medicine career advising, in partnership with the Office of Student Experience including: Advising Emergency Medicine–interested students throughout all four years and guiding MS4 students through residency applications and away rotations. Completing Standardized Letters of Evaluation (SLOEs) for all Emergency Medicine–interested students. Assisting non-matching students through SOAP and the Scramble. Maintaining relationships with Emergency Medicine residency programs in the Pacific Northwest and nationally, and participating in Society for Academic Emergency Medicine (SAEM) activities related to undergraduate Emergency Medicine education and the Match. Non-Essential Functions | 5% Performs other related duties and responsibilities as needed. Additional Information: Screening of application will begin October 28, 2026. This is a full-time , continuous career track position. This position is overtime exempt. Annual Salary: The base salary range is commensurate with qualifications, track and rank determination. Compensation at 100% FTE is as follows: Assistant Rank: $222,900 - $232,900 | Commensurate with qualifications Associate Rank: $245,190 - $255,190 | Commensurate with qualifications Full Professor Rank: $269,709 - $279,709 | Commensurate with qualifications   While serving as Vice Chair, this position will be eligible for an administrative stipend of $30,000. to be renewed annually. In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the above salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position. Successful candidates typically begin up to mid-point of the salary range. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick and vacation leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts. For additional information, please review the detailed Summary of Benefits offered by WSU for Faculty and Total Compensation . Required Qualifications for all Candidates: Doctor of Medicine, Doctor of Osteopathic Medicine or equivalent degree with Board Certification in Emergency Medicine through ABEM. Three or more years of clinical patient care experience in their specialty. Three or more years of experience teaching, developing, evaluating or leading educational programs at the undergraduate or graduate medical education level. Required Qualifications for Candidates at Associate Rank: Current or previous appointment at the Associate rank or equivalent at a post-secondary institution; OR experience determined to be equivalent. Record of accomplishments applicable to the sub-track in teaching, research, clinical, scholarship and/or service activities commensurate with school, college, and university standards for associate rank. Additional Required Qualifications for Candidates at Full Professor Rank: Current or previous appointment at the full professor rank or equivalent at a post-secondary institution; OR experience determined to be equivalent. Record of accomplishments applicable to the sub-track in teaching, research, clinical, scholarship, and/or service activities commensurate with school, college, and university standards for full professor rank.   Preferred Qualifications for all Candidates: Experience developing medical school clinical courses. Experience leading or managing professionals in a complex organization. Proven experience in management of multiple initiatives and multiple sites. Academic faculty status at allopathic or osteopathic medical school. Experience with LCME and/or other accrediting bodies. Experience working with diverse students or diverse populations, especially in rural and under-served areas. Position Sponsorship Eligibility: May be eligible for work visa sponsorship. Area/College: Elson S. Floyd College of Medicine Department Name: Department of Medical Education and Clinical Sciences Location: This position may be based at any of the Elson S. Floyd College of Medicine Campus locations in Washington State, such as Spokane, Tri-Cities, Vancouver or Everett. Background Check: This position has been designated by the department to require a background check because it requires access to children or vulnerable adults as defined by RCW 74.34 , engages in law enforcement, requires security clearance, interacts with WSU students in a counseling or advising capacity, has access to personal identifying and/or financial information, unsupervised access to university buildings/property, or other business-related need. A background check will not be completed until an initial determination of qualification for employment has been made. Application Instructions: As part of your application materials, review the Leadership and Management Principles outlined below. Reflect on these principles by describing specific examples from your experience that demonstrate how you have applied them in practice and in your leadership approach. Applicants must upload the following required documents to their online application. Application materials should clearly communicate how the applicant meets all required qualifications and additional requirements. Reference contact information will be requested later int he recruitment process through Workday. 1) Resume/CV 2) Cover Letter 3) Leadership and Management Principles Document External candidates , upload all documents in the “Application Document” section of your application. Current WSU Employees (internal candidates) , before starting your Workday application, please use these instructions to update your education and experience in your worker profile in Workday . Internal candidates, upload all documents in the “Resume/Cover Letter” section of your application. Documents may be submitted as one file or separate files. Applicants are encouraged to upload as a PDF, if possible. Reference contact information will be requested later in the recruitment process through Workday. Leadership and Management Principles: 1. Mission-Driven Decision Making Regularly aligning work with the goals of the team, office, and College of Medicine for decision making. Taking ownership of organizational decisions and creating positive visions for employees and colleagues.   2. Strategic Collaboration Creating, leading, and engaging cross-functional initiatives and work groups to solve shared challenges. Sharing credit and celebrating joint successes Being willing to lead, co-lead, follow, defer, and co-create to best fit the situation.   3. Adaptive Leadership Taking a stance of humility and openness to personal growth and learning. Inviting alternative perspectives and adapting work approaches based on feedback. Encouraging experimentation and learning from setbacks without blame.   4. Empathetic Communication Actively listening without interrupting and summarizing what you heard. Using clear, audience – focused language in emails and meetings, even under pressure. Communicating as transparently as possible during change, explaining the “why” behind decisions. Pro-actively engaging in repair when communication fails.   5. Ownership and Integrity Following through on commitments and owning mistakes. Setting clear expectations for yourself and measuring progress against stated goals. Ensuring transparency in budget and resource allocation. Demonstrate global perspective and leadership in all interactions.   6. Talent Development and Empowerment Setting clear expectations and measuring progress against stated goals. Recognizing contributions and encouraging others to do the same. Proactively engaging employees in their professional development and career growth. Giving clear, actionable feedback and supporting growth of direct reports. Checking in on team well-being during high-stress periods. Time Type: Full time Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
    },
    "https://wsu.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/WSU_Jobs/job/Spokane-WA/Clinical-Assistant-Professor---Career---College-of-Nursing_R-15452": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant Professor - Career | College of Nursing",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\nFull time\n\nOnline applications must be received before 11:59pm on: October 21, 2026 If a date is not listed above, review the Applicant Instructions below for more details. Available Title(s): 164-NN_FACULTY - Clinical Assistant Professor - Career, 396-NN_FACULTY - New Teaching Track - Pre-Academic Year Business Title: Clinical Assistant Professor - Career | College of Nursing Employee Type: Faculty (+) (Fixed Term) Position Term: 9 Month - Summer Position Details: The Opportunity: Washington State University and the College of Nursing invite applications for a full-time, 9-month, career-track clinician-scholar-educator who holds current national certification as a Family Nurse Practitioner. As a Clinical Assistant Professor in the College of Nursing, you will primarily work on the WSU Spokane campus . In this position, you will support the College’s mission to prepare practice-ready nurses and advanced practice providers who improve health and access to care across Washington and beyond. You will contribute to teaching, clinical evaluation, mentorship, scholarship, service, and academic-practice partnerships that support the Family Nurse Practitioner program and broader College of Nursing priorities. Teach doctoral, master’s, and/or undergraduate nursing students in classroom, online, simulation, and clinical learning environments. Advise and mentor undergraduate and/or graduate nursing students. Serve as a clinical faculty evaluator for Family Nurse Practitioner students in clinical settings. Maintain an active program of clinical scholarship and disseminate scholarly work through appropriate professional venues. Serve on graduate student committees and contribute to curriculum, assessment, and program improvement activities. Provide service to the department, College of Nursing, university, profession, and community. Engage with health care agencies and community partners to support FNP clinical placement opportunities and strengthen academic-practice partnerships. Participate in clinical practice through collaborating community clinics when aligned with workload, expertise, and program need. Additional Information: The screening of applicants begins October 14, 2026. This is a full-time (100% FTE), 9-month, career-track faculty appointment. The position is not eligible for overtime . The position may include a hybrid work arrangement; however, the faculty member is expected to be on campus as needed to support teaching, clinical activities, student engagement, meetings, and other core responsibilities. 9-month Academic Salary: $91,394 - $94,546 | Commensurate with experience and qualifications In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the above salary reflects the full salary range for this position. Individual placement within the range is based on the candidate’s current experience, education, skills, and abilities related to the position. Successful candidates typically begin up to mid-point of the salary range. Benefits: WSU offers a comprehensive benefits package which includes paid sick and vacation leave; paid holidays; medical, dental, life and disability insurance package for employees and dependents; retirement; deferred compensation, optional supplemental retirement accounts. For additional information, please review the detailed Summary of Benefits offered by WSU for Faculty and Total Compensation . Required Qualifications: DNP or PhD in nursing or a closely related field by date of hire. Current national certification as a Family Nurse Practitioner. At least two years of full-time clinical practice experience as a Family Nurse Practitioner. Meets eligibility requirements for registered nurse licensure and advanced registered nurse practitioner licensure with prescriptive authority in Washington State or through the Multi-State License, if not already licensed. Demonstrated commitment to teaching, mentoring, and supporting students from varied backgrounds. Evidence of clinical expertise, effective communication, and the ability to work collaboratively with students, faculty, staff, and clinical partners. Preferred Qualifications: Experience teaching in graduate nursing education, including FNP or advanced practice nursing coursework. Experience evaluating students in clinical practice settings. Experience developing or maintaining clinical partnerships and placement opportunities. Record of clinical scholarship, quality improvement, practice innovation, or professional presentations/publications. Experience with interprofessional education or team-based care. Position Sponsorship Eligibility: Not eligible for work visa sponsorship Area/College: College of Nursing Department Name: Advanced Practice and Community-Based Care Location: Health Sciences Campus - Spokane, WA Application Instructions: Applicants must upload the following required documents to their online application. 1) Curriculum Vitae 2) A cover letter describing your preparation for teaching and mentoring nursing students, experience as a Family Nurse Practitioner; clinical scholarship interests, and commitment to the mission of the WSU College of Nursing. 3) Teaching Statement 4) Contact information of at least four professional references External candidates , upload all documents in the “Application Document” section of your application. Current WSU Employees (internal candidates) , before starting your Workday application, please use these instructions to update your education and experience in your worker profile in Workday . Internal candidates, upload all documents in the “Resume/Cover Letter” section of your application. Documents may be submitted as one file or separate files. Applicants are encouraged to upload as a PDF, if possible.   About WSU, College of Nursing, and Spokane: The Washington State University College of Nursing is a prestigious institution offering accredited BSN, MN, DNP, and PhD programs. We strive to transform healthcare and uphold core values of integrity, caring, and social justice. With campuses in Spokane, Tri-Cities, and Vancouver, we cater to community needs and seek individuals who can make a positive impact on nursing. This position will serve on the Health Sciences campus in Spokane, WA with the colleges of Nursing, Pharmacy and Pharmaceutical Sciences, and Medicine co-located here. The health sciences programs collaborate to offer interprofessional learning for all students. The WSU Health Sciences Spokane campus is minutes from Spokane’s Riverfront Park, with easy access to the downtown area along the Centennial Trail. Spokane has the second-largest urban waterfall in the United States, which is just minutes from campus. There are more than 75 lakes and rivers within a one-hour drive of Spokane, including the beautiful Coeur d’Alene Lake. Background Check: This position has been designated by the department to require a background check because it requires access to children or vulnerable adults as defined by RCW 74.34 , engages in law enforcement, requires security clearance, interacts with WSU students in a counseling or advising capacity, has access to personal identifying and/or financial information, unsupervised access to university buildings/property, or other business-related need. A background check will not be completed until an initial determination of qualification for employment has been made. Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/ Time Type: Full time Washington State University encourages all qualified candidates to apply, including members of the military and veterans, and persons with disabilities. WSU employs only U.S. citizens and lawfully authorized non-U.S. citizens. All new employees must show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services. WSU is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact Human Resource Services: 509-335-4521 (v), Washington State TDD Relay Service: Voice Callers: 1-800-833-6384; TDD Callers: 1-800-833-6388, 509-335-1259(f), or hrs@wsu.edu . Notice of Non-Discrimination In matters of admission, employment, housing or services, or in the educational programs or activities it operates, WSU does not discriminate or permit discrimination by any member of its community against any individual on the basis of sex, race, color, national or ethnic origin, physical disability, mental disability, sensory disability, use of a trained service animal, age, religion, creed, genetic information, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, protected veteran status, honorably discharged veteran, member of the military, or immigration or citizenship status except as authorized by federal or state law, regulation, or government contract. Inquiries about the application of federal and state laws and regulations (including inquiries regarding the application of Title IX, ADA/Section 504, and implementing regulations), as well as reports and complaints of discrimination and harassment, can be made verbally or in writing to WSU’s Compliance and Civil Rights, the Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights, or both. More information, resources, and contact information are available here: https://ccr.wsu.edu/notice-of-nondiscrimination/"
